    {
     "type": "table",
     "title": "Dativ articles",
     "skipFirst": true,
     "intro": "Tap a row to hear it.",
     "cols": [
      "",
      "Nominativ",
      "Dativ"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "der (m)",
       "der / ein Mann",
       "dem / einem Mann"
      ],
      [
       "die (f)",
       "die / eine Frau",
       "der / einer Frau"
      ],
      [
       "das (n)",
       "das / ein Kind",
       "dem / einem Kind"
      ],
      [
       "plural",
       "die Kinder",
       "den Kindern"
      ]
     ],
     "note": "Masculine and neuter are the same (**dem**). In the plural the noun usually gets an extra **-n**: *den Kinder**n***. Pronouns: *ich → mir, du → dir*."
    },
    {
     "type": "note",
     "title": "When do you need the Dativ?",
     "md": "1. After certain **prepositions** (lesson 30, and *mit*, *zu* from lessons 27–28).\n2. After certain **verbs** — about 50, e.g. **helfen**, **gehören**: {de:Der Mann hilft der Frau.} · {de:Der Stift gehört mir.}\n3. With **two objects** — the thing is Akkusativ, the **person who receives** is Dativ:\n\n{de:Der Mann gibt **der Frau** **einen Brief**.} → *der Frau* (to whom?) = Dativ, *einen Brief* (what?) = Akkusativ.\n\nWith two nouns, the **Dativ usually comes first**."
    },
